REVIEWER: Because `rust_fft_` is initialized in the member initializer list, `rustfft_new(fft_size_)` executes before the constructor body's `CHECK_GE(fft_size_, MinFFTSize())`. If an invalid size (like 0) is passed, `rustfft_new` attempts `(0 - 1) / 2` on `usize`, causing an arithmetic underflow panic in Rust rather than hitting the C++ `CHECK_GE`.

Would it make sense to validate `fft_size` before initializing `rust_fft_`?

```suggestion
namespace {

unsigned CheckFftSize(unsigned fft_size) {
  CHECK_GE(fft_size, FFTFrame::MinFFTSize());
  CHECK_LE(fft_size, FFTFrame::MaxFFTSize());
  return fft_size;
}

}  // namespace

FFTFrame::FFTFrame(unsigned fft_size)
    : fft_size_(CheckFftSize(fft_size)),
      rust_fft_(blink::rust_fft::rustfft_new(fft_size_)) {
  const unsigned packed_size = (fft_size_ + 1) / 2;
  real_data_.Allocate(packed_size);
  imag_data_.Allocate(packed_size);
}
```
